The desktop agent's parked commit `bf47e441`, read hunk by hunk and verified against the tip before
applying. All three were needed. Two were strengthened, one gained a guard, and none of it is a rebase
of their commit: their field evidence drove the change, the code is this branch's.

**1. `CRUSORAI_UI_STAMP` 2026-09-11 → 2026-09-26 (`crusorAIRenderer.ts`).** The chip reads
`ui <stamp>` and appends STALE when the engine build date is newer, so forgetting the bump makes a
freshly compiled workbench accuse itself of being old — which is what the owner saw. The convention is
documented in the pins that must move with it, `test_ui_stamp_bumped_for_the_*_round`; the preview
chrome landed 2026-09-26 and the stamp did not move, so both pins were still asserting the old value.
The pins are updated, the round is named, and the docstring says why.

**2. stdout/stderr reconfigured with `encoding="utf-8", errors="replace"`.** Complements the existing
`line_buffering=True` — now the same call, so the two cannot drift apart. This is a real kill, not a
hypothetical: `render()` prints `[tool done] name → result`, and cp1252 (a Windows console when
redirected) cannot encode `→`, `…` or curly quotes. Reproduced here: with the encoding fix removed the
child dies with `UnicodeEncodeError: 'charmap' codec can't encode character '\u2192'` — byte for byte
the owner's run-1 kill. `errors="replace"` degrades such a character to `?` instead.

**3. A fresh session id per invocation.** Their diagnosis is right, and the engine agrees: the bridge
keys durable state in `~/.crusorai/sessions.db` by session id (`_prior_checkpoint_count` counts it per
`thread_id`), and the engine's own note records this class from a benchmark — "a fixed id made each
benchmark run replay every prior run (+3 calls / +~10k tokens per run, linear)". `batch_session_id()`
returns `preview-batch-turn-<pid>-<ns>`: `pid + int(time.time())` (as patched) can repeat within a
second, so the clock is nanosecond. The harness prints the id, and now **refuses to be silent about a
resume**: `session_info` carries `prior_checkpoints`, and a non-zero count prints SESSION NOT FRESH —
a run that replays history proves nothing about the prompt, which is this harness's entire purpose.

Also: `demo_batch()`'s own `sessionId` field is payload shape only (`build_prompt_frame` uses its
argument, pinned in test_preview_prompt.py), commented so nobody "fixes" the wrong line.

5 new pins (17 in the file): the cp1252 child asserts stdout AND stderr return as UTF-8 with the arrow
intact and `line_buffering` still True; two separate invocations must not share an id; a scripted
bridge proves a resumed session is shouted about and a fresh one is not. Each was checked against the
behaviour it replaces — no encoding → `UnicodeEncodeError`; a fixed id → "two invocations shared a
session id"; warning silenced → the guard fails.

236 passed in the preview+bridge+desktop sweep. The 6 failures there are pre-existing and identical at
the previous tip (verified in a pristine worktree): 4 are sandbox venv gaps (`langgraph`,
`typing_extensions`) and 2 are the tracked `out/` being stale against the current contrib sources —
which is also why the chip reads an old stamp, and which the §5 rebuild regenerates.

Runbook: §6 gained the chip row (it must read `ui 2026-09-26` with no STALE after the rebuild), §7.5
explains why every run is a fresh session.

